namespace molpro{
namespace linalg{
/*!
@page TopicArrayHandlers Array handlers

Work in Progress.

@section ArrayHandlersContiguousDisk Contiguous arrays and disk arrays

When the R space is held in contiguous memory, e.g. @c std::vector<double> or array::Span<double>, and the Q space on
disk, e.g. array::DistrArrayFile, the default handlers are array::ArrayHandlerIterableDDisk (rq) and
array::ArrayHandlerDDiskIterable (qr). Every operation is available in both directions.

- The contiguous array is addressed in place; there are no per-element proxies.
- The disk array is paged through @c get() and @c put(), so that the memory used does not scale with its size. gemm
  reads it through a double-buffered array::util::BufferManager and does the arithmetic with dgemm. R vectors that
  are not evenly spaced in memory are first copied, a page at a time, into one buffer, which each thread keeps for
  later calls.
- A Q made as a copy of an R by the qr handler is created on molpro::mpi::comm_self(), because R is not distributed.
  The directory for its file, or a function that creates the copy, can be given to the handler's constructor. As for
  any distributed array on a communicator other than molpro::mpi::comm_global(), MPI must have been initialised.
- If a Q is distributed over several processes, the R arrays hold all elements on every process. Each process works
  on the local section of Q, and an R written to is then replicated over all processes.

The handlers forward to array::DistrArray members taking a Span, e.g. @c dot(Span<const double>), and to the
contiguous gemm functions in array::util, so the same operations are available for any distributed array.

@subsection ArrayHandlersChoosingR Choosing the R array type

- On a single process, or with an independent solve on each process, a contiguous R such as @c std::vector<double>
  or array::Span<double> is the simplest choice. It needs no distribution or communicator, and performs as well as
  array::DistrArraySpan.
- For a solve shared by several processes, use a distributed R, e.g. array::DistrArraySpan, distributed like Q.
  Each process then does only its share of the work on R, and nothing has to be gathered after an operation. A
  contiguous R is replicated instead: every operation on R alone is done in full on every process, and every
  operation that writes R ends with an @c MPI_Allgatherv over Q's communicator.
- If the caller holds full vectors on every process, wrap the local section of each in an array::DistrArraySpan and
  gather the vectors once when they are handed back, as the C and Fortran interface does
  (@c CreateDistrArray and @c DistrArraySynchronize in IterativeSolverCMPI.cpp).

*/
}
}
